<p>Public prices and limits were checked on October 8, 2026. USD amounts are stated only where the billing context was verified. Taxes, payment processing, messaging credits and other add-ons can change total cost. This comparison uses official sources and does not claim measured booking conversion or no-show improvements.</p>
<h2>What TidyCal already offers</h2>
<p>TidyCal's Free plan includes unlimited bookings, unlimited booking types and paid bookings. Individual Lifetime is advertised at $29 one time; Agency Lifetime at $79; Pro at $12 monthly or $99 yearly. Agency includes round-robin and collective meetings. Treat these as current offers with different entitlements, rather than one universal lifetime package. <a href="https://tidycal.com/pricing">TidyCal plans</a></p>
<p>The same page lists a 1% platform fee on Stripe-paid bookings for Free and lifetime plans, in addition to Stripe's own charges. Pro removes that platform fee. Calendar connections and SMS allowances also vary; Agency's included SMS is limited and covers US and Canadian numbers.</p>
<p>Keep TidyCal when the existing booking flow fits your needs. A paid-for lifetime license has no new subscription charge, and an upgrade within TidyCal may address the missing feature more economically than moving. Its current capabilities are broader than a booking-page-only description suggests. <a href="https://tidycal.com/">TidyCal overview</a></p>
<p>The right reason to switch is a verified requirement: a particular integration, communication workflow, service model or team policy. Do not migrate because a comparison says a feature is absent without checking today's plan.</p>
<h2>How to choose a replacement</h2>
<p>These six options cover different booking jobs. The selection favors calendar connections, availability controls, communication, host licensing and appointment operations. A solo host, a sales team and a business with staff providers should not receive the same recommendation.</p>
<p>Write down the requirement in one sentence: “Check both work and personal calendars,” “assign a lead to one available colleague,” or “book a service with the correct staff member.” Then find the plan that supports it and price that plan.</p>
<p>Also distinguish reading a calendar for conflicts from writing a new booking into it. An app may check several calendars but put new events in one destination. Both behaviors need verification in your real account.</p>
<h2>1. Cal.com: a strong hosted individual starting point</h2>
<p>Cal.com's hosted Free plan includes one user with unlimited event types and calendar connections. That makes it worth evaluating when one host has several meeting types and calendars, and wants to start with a working individual plan. <a href="https://cal.com/pricing">Cal.com pricing</a></p>
<p>For example, a consultant can assess whether separate introductory and existing-client appointments can share the right availability rules. This is a proposed use case, not a claim that all calendar providers behave identically.</p>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> individual Free is the baseline. Teams is displayed at $12 per user per month under yearly billing, totaling $144 per user annually, with round-robin, collective event types and routing forms. Connected services and messaging may have separate terms.</p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> team scheduling creates a per-host commitment. Compare the actual number of people who need paid access against TidyCal's existing entitlement, rather than comparing a free solo tier with a team plan.</p>
<p>Cal.com's commercial production service also differs from Cal.diy, its separate open-source self-hosting project. The official technical explanation says team and organization capabilities were removed from the community edition. Self-hosting is a separate operational decision, not a way to assume hosted team features are free. <a href="https://cal.com/blog/cal-diy-open-source-to-closed-source">Cal.com and Cal.diy separation</a></p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> TidyCal already handles the same individual workflow, or the reason to switch depends on unverified feature parity with self-hosted software.</p>
<p>Evaluate one conflict across two accounts and one reschedule before replacing your public link.</p>
<h2>2. Calendly: integrated meeting and team workflows</h2>
<p>Calendly is relevant when booking meetings connects to a wider business process. Its documented plans distinguish basic individual scheduling from reminders, integrations, routing and centrally managed team events. <a href="https://calendly.com/">Calendly overview</a></p>
<p>A team that needs different meeting rules for qualification and handoff should evaluate those requirements together. The value is the working process, not the brand's familiarity.</p>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> Free includes one event type and one calendar connection. Standard is $10 per seat per month billed yearly, or $120 per host annually, with more event types, calendar connections, reminders and payment connections. Teams is $16 per seat per month billed yearly, or $192 annually, for team scheduling and routing. Invitees do not require a paid seat. <a href="https://calendly.com/pricing">Calendly plan comparison</a></p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> the price is per host. Several paid hosts can make the total materially different from a solo comparison. Optional AI tools shown on the pricing page also should not be assumed to be part of the base scheduling quote.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> you want several event types on a free solo page, or the current TidyCal plan covers your requirements without another recurring bill.</p>
<p>Check the complete host list, calendar conflicts and workflow steps. If an integration is the reason for switching, verify the particular action and its plan access. A logo on an integrations page does not prove it replaces your existing automation.</p>
<h2>3. SavvyCal: pay attention to the invitee's experience</h2>
<p>SavvyCal's distinguishing feature is the recipient's ability to overlay their calendar on the booking view. It also documents preferred times, buffers and limits. This is relevant when finding a mutually convenient time matters more than simply exposing every available slot. <a href="https://savvycal.com/">SavvyCal features</a></p>
<p>Consider a host arranging interviews with busy external participants. Evaluate whether the booking experience makes the intended times easier to see, without claiming a conversion improvement this article has not measured.</p>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> own active scheduling links require paid access. Free availability sharing and guest participation are different from running your own public booking workflow. Round-robin participation also requires paid access. <a href="https://docs.savvycal.com/article/96-sharing-availability">SavvyCal access distinctions</a></p>
<p>The current pricing page separates Basic from Premium; paid bookings, custom domains and delegation appear in Premium. Numeric amounts were displayed with an unresolved billing selector, so no reliable monthly or annual quote is asserted here. <a href="https://savvycal.com/pricing">SavvyCal plans</a></p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> its interface advantages may not justify a subscription if you mainly want occasional paid appointments. Check the exact Premium requirement rather than assuming Basic includes every highlighted feature.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> your priority is a permanently free own booking page, or your needed provider connection is not verified.</p>
<p>Evaluate both sides of the flow: what you configure and what a recipient sees. Calendar overlay only helps if the participant chooses to use it.</p>
<h2>4. YouCanBookMe: tailor the page and communication</h2>
<p>YouCanBookMe is relevant when the booking page, form questions and confirmation messages need to reflect a particular workflow. Its product and pricing pages describe configurable booking pages, communication and calendar connections. <a href="https://youcanbook.me/">YouCanBookMe overview</a></p>
<p>A consultant with different instructions for new and returning clients can evaluate whether the page collects the right information and sends useful preparation details. This should be assessed as one end-to-end appointment flow.</p>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> Free has one booking page and one calendar connection. Paid tiers add more pages and connections; Professional documents six connections, ten pages and expanded workflow controls. Paid card amounts rendered as zero placeholders during research, so their real public rates are not verified. <a href="https://youcanbook.me/pricing">YouCanBookMe plans</a></p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> calendar-count, page-count, messaging and payment conditions need pricing together. Do not interpret a broken paid-price display as a zero-cost professional plan. SMS and payments can also carry additional fees.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> you mainly need a no-extra-cost replacement for an already owned lifetime booking tool and the customization adds little practical value.</p>
<p>Configure one form and examine how its answers appear in the confirmation and calendar event. A page that collects information is only useful if the people handling the appointment receive it in the right place.</p>
<h2>5. SimplyBook.me: appointments for a service business</h2>
<p>SimplyBook.me is a broader appointment platform for a business organizing services and providers. It is worth considering when the booking is an operational service appointment, rather than merely a link for a meeting with one host. <a href="https://simplybook.me/en/">SimplyBook.me overview</a></p>
<p>For example, a studio may need the client to choose a service and a suitable staff provider. Provider capacity and service configuration are then part of the replacement decision.</p>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> Free includes 50 bookings per month, one provider and one premium custom feature, and requires monthly renewal. Paid plans scale booking volume, provider count and active premium features. Because the extracted pricing page did not establish the currency code for paid rates, this guide does not treat a dollar symbol as a verified USD quote. <a href="https://simplybook.me/en/pricing">SimplyBook.me pricing</a></p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> a feature allowance can force a plan change even when booking volume is low. The pricing page also says custom domains, SMS gateways and SMS credits are paid separately. Match the service configuration before comparing totals.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> you only need a meeting link and would leave the service-business tools unused.</p>
<p>Build one realistic service with its duration, provider and payment conditions. Confirm how a cancellation affects availability. Avoid assuming a richer platform is automatically a better fit for a simple consultation.</p>
<h2>6. Google Calendar appointment schedules: an existing-account baseline</h2>
<p>Google Calendar is worth checking before buying another scheduler if your meetings already live in Google. A personal account and Workspace Business Starter can create one booking page, while premium features depend on the subscription. Some Workspace editions do not support appointment schedules. <a href="https://support.google.com/calendar/answer/16287038?hl=en">Google eligibility comparison</a></p>
<p>The useful case is one uncomplicated meeting type with an existing calendar. You reduce the number of services you administer if the built-in page already does what you need.</p>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> a basic personal booking page can be a no-extra-cost starting point. Additional schedules, automatic reminders and multiple-calendar availability require eligible access. Payment collection is not included in every consumer subscription, so check the exact column in Google's eligibility table.</p>
<p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> customization is narrower than a dedicated communication workflow. Google's setup help says reminder email text cannot be edited. Creating the schedule also uses a supported computer browser. <a href="https://support.google.com/calendar/answer/10729749?hl=en">Appointment schedule setup</a></p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> you need an independently verified round-robin process, extensive page communication, or a non-Google calendar workflow.</p>
<p>Check the account you will actually use. Access in a personal account does not establish the same capability in an employer-managed account.</p>
<h2>Compare the full cost of the needed feature</h2>
<p>Use the same period and host count. Compare an annual commitment with another annual total, then consider whether a monthly plan is preferable while evaluating. An already purchased lifetime plan is a sunk purchase; assess the new cost of staying against the new cost of switching.</p>
<p>Include the payment processor, any platform percentage, messaging and the tools needed for the complete workflow. Do not assume a platform's no-fee statement removes a processor's charges. For a team, distinguish paid organizers from guests and staff-provider bundles.</p>
<p>A useful price comparison contains the plan, hosts or providers, calendar limits and required add-ons. It does not need a speculative estimate of recovered revenue or prevented missed appointments.</p>
<h2>Switch without losing existing booking links</h2>
<p>Export records first where available. TidyCal documents CSV export for contacts and bookings on Individual, Agency and Pro, but not Free. A CSV preserves records; it does not automatically recreate templates, payment settings or future cancellation links in another service. <a href="https://help.tidycal.com/article/722-tidycal-is-gdpr-compliant">TidyCal export help</a></p>
<p>Rebuild one booking type with the same duration, notice period, buffers, destination calendar and questions. Make a controlled booking, reschedule it and cancel it. Confirm the notification, calendar event and availability change at each step.</p>
<p>Inventory links in your website, email signature, social profiles and automated messages. Replace those after the new flow passes your checks. Existing TidyCal bookings may still depend on their original rescheduling links, so keep that account available while they run their course.</p>
<p>Avoid importing every future event as a new booking without checking the destination's behavior. Duplicate appointments and reminders are possible when both systems are active. Retire the old public booking flow after the replacement is ready, then handle subscription cancellation separately from account deletion.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Which TidyCal alternative has a useful free individual plan?</h3>
<p>Cal.com includes one user with unlimited calendars and event types. YouCanBookMe and Calendly have free plans with narrower page or event limits. Choose based on the required calendar connections and booking types.</p>
<h3>Does TidyCal support paid bookings?</h3>
<p>Yes, its current pricing page lists paid bookings on Free. Platform and processor fees still matter. Switching solely to gain payments may be unnecessary.</p>
<h3>Is TidyCal Lifetime the same as Pro?</h3>
<p>No. Individual and Agency lifetime offers differ from the recurring Pro plan. Pro includes its own branding, domain, calendar and platform-fee entitlements.</p>
<h3>Is hosted Cal.com still the same as the open-source version?</h3>
<p>No. Cal.com documents a separation from Cal.diy, the community self-hosting project. Team and organization features are among the removed commercial capabilities; verify the edition you intend to use.</p>
<h3>Which option fits staff service appointments?</h3>
<p>SimplyBook.me is the service-business candidate in this shortlist. Assess provider counts, services, bookings and premium-feature limits together. A solo meeting link may require less software.</p>
<h3>Will my existing bookings move automatically?</h3>
<p>No complete transfer from TidyCal to these vendors was verified. Preserve records and check future appointments separately, including the original cancellation and rescheduling links.</p>
<h2>Start with one complete booking flow</h2>
<p>Choose the replacement that covers the feature causing friction, then price the correct plan for your actual hosts. Rebuild one booking type and follow it through confirmation, rescheduling and cancellation. Keep TidyCal if an existing plan or sensible upgrade already meets the requirement. Switch after the new flow works and the extra cost has a defined purpose.</p>
